Aim: Deadlock Avoidance using Banker's Algorithm – Write a program to simulate the Banker's algorithm for deadlock prevention.

Theory:
1. What is Deadlock?
A deadlock is a situation in an operating system where two or more processes wait indefinitely for resources held by each other. As a result, none of the processes can continue execution.
2. What is Banker's Algorithm?
Banker's Algorithm is a deadlock avoidance algorithm used in operating systems. It checks whether allocating resources to a process will keep the system in a safe state. If the system remains safe, the resources are allocated; otherwise, the request is postponed.
3. Important Terms:
Allocation:	Resources currently allocated to each process
Max:	Maximum resources each process may need
Need:	Remaining resources required by a process
Available:	Resources currently available
Safe Sequence: Order in which processes can complete safely.

Code:
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int allocation[3][3] = {
        {1, 1, 0},
        {2, 0, 1},
        {1, 1, 1}
    };

    int max[3][3] = {
        {2, 2, 1},
        {3, 1, 2},
        {2, 2, 2}
    };

    int available[3] = {2, 1, 1};

    int need[3][3];
    int finish[3] = {0, 0, 0};
    int safe[3];  // Safe Sequence Array 

    int i, j, k = 0;
    int canFinish;  //check whether a process can finish.

    // Calculate Need
    for (i = 0; i < 3; i++)
    {
        for (j = 0; j < 3; j++)
        {
            need[i][j] = max[i][j] - allocation[i][j];
        }
    }

    // Banker's Algorithm
    while (k < 3)
    {
        for (i = 0; i < 3; i++)
        {
            if (finish[i] == 0)
            {
                canFinish = 1;

                for (j = 0; j < 3; j++)
                {
                    if (need[i][j] > available[j])
                    {
                        canFinish = 0;
                    }
                }

                if (canFinish == 1)
                {
                    safe[k] = i;  //stores the process in the safe sequence.
                    k++;

                    // Release resources
                    for (j = 0; j < 3; j++)
                    {
                        available[j] += allocation[i][j];
                    }

                    finish[i] = 1;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    printf("Safe Sequence: ");

    for (i = 0; i < 3; i++)
    {
        printf("P%d", safe[i]);

        if (i < 2)
            printf(" -> ");
    }

    printf("\n");

    return 0;
}


Output: Safe Sequence: P0 -> P1 -> P2
